/T (to struggle within a seemingly endless mental prison. For example, some individuals with OCD will ruminate tirelessly on the question of whether they wish to harm their family and closest friends, and experience extreme anxiety at the prospect that these thoughts may be indicative of a genuine desire. This sub variant of OCD, known as Harm-OCD, is one of many examples of a lesser known type of the disorder called Pure-Obsessional OCD, or Pure-O. Pure-O is mechanically identically to classic OCD presentations, although the compulsions used to counteract anxiety \(see below\) are almost entirely mental. )
/E (to struggle within a seemingly endless mental prison. For example, some individuals with OCD will ruminate tirelessly on the question of whether they wish to harm their family and closest friends, and experience extreme anxiety at the prospect that these thoughts may be indicative of a genuine desire. This sub variant of OCD, known as Harm-OCD, is one of many examples of a lesser known type of the disorder called Pure-Obsessional OCD, or Pure-O. Pure-O is mechanically identically to classic OCD presentations, although the compulsions used to counteract anxiety \(see below\) are almost entirely mental. )
