416 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 201 0 R
/T (The following theoretical model by Wilhelm and Steketee \(2006\) provides a succinct overview for how OCD can form, and how symptoms are maintained. Recalling the earlier points on the genetic factors influencing the disorder's development, we can see that these factors combine with life experiences and personality traits/core beliefs to shape our interpretations of the intrusive thoughts we experience. To a greater extent, our interpretations of obsessive thoughts are what begins and sustains our suffering, which is why a substantial component of therapy is centered on changing our responses to thoughts we've labelled, or 'flagged', as dangerous and unacceptable.)
/E (The following theoretical model by Wilhelm and Steketee \(2006\) provides a succinct overview for how OCD can form, and how symptoms are maintained. Recalling the earlier points on the genetic factors influencing the disorder's development, we can see that these factors combine with life experiences and personality traits/core beliefs to shape our interpretations of the intrusive thoughts we experience. To a greater extent, our interpretations of obsessive thoughts are what begins and sustains our suffering, which is why a substantial component of therapy is centered on changing our responses to thoughts we've labelled, or 'flagged', as dangerous and unacceptable.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
