/T (According to the model, personality traits \(such as perfectionism\)  influence the frequency and intensity of intrusive thoughts, and  mould our natural inclinations to respond to them in certain ways. After we've labelled a particular thought as harmful, unacceptable, or outright 'wrong', this typically produces a strong negative emotional reaction, or series of negative emotional reactions, including anxiety, dread, sadness, and depression. To regulate these emotions, that is, to attempt to get rid of or reduce them, we then engage in any number of compulsions or safety behaviours, which usually alleviate some immediate distress at the cost of our long-term recovery.)
/E (According to the model, personality traits \(such as perfectionism\)  influence the frequency and intensity of intrusive thoughts, and  mould our natural inclinations to respond to them in certain ways. After we've labelled a particular thought as harmful, unacceptable, or outright 'wrong', this typically produces a strong negative emotional reaction, or series of negative emotional reactions, including anxiety, dread, sadness, and depression. To regulate these emotions, that is, to attempt to get rid of or reduce them, we then engage in any number of compulsions or safety behaviours, which usually alleviate some immediate distress at the cost of our long-term recovery.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [6]
>>
