/T (Distress tolerance can be defined by an individual's capacity to tolerate distress, be it psychological, emotional or physical distress. In the context of OCD recovery, distress refers to the emotional distress associated with anxiety. Anxiety in and of itself is not a bad thing - it's adaptive, and can keep you safe when occurring at appropriate times. It can also shelter you within a constricting and suffocating 'comfort zone', taking from you almost everything, including your peace.)
/E (Distress tolerance can be defined by an individual's capacity to tolerate distress, be it psychological, emotional or physical distress. In the context of OCD recovery, distress refers to the emotional distress associated with anxiety. Anxiety in and of itself is not a bad thing - it's adaptive, and can keep you safe when occurring at appropriate times. It can also shelter you within a constricting and suffocating 'comfort zone', taking from you almost everything, including your peace.)
/Pg 33 0 R
