671 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 217 0 R
/T (Firstly, you absolutely can stop - this I can confidently assure you of. As we've outlined in previous modules, you brain has paired the compulsion, regardless of what kind you use, with relief. Relief from pain, be it physical or emotional pain, is a powerful reinforcer for behaviour, which is, in some ways, how addiction can start. You can think of compulsions as an addiction of sorts, as the rush of relief floods your nervous system with a wave of the reward-hormone dopamine. This same hormone is released when we anticipate a reward, consume drugs or alcohol, and achieve a goal or objective.)
/E (Firstly, you absolutely can stop - this I can confidently assure you of. As we've outlined in previous modules, you brain has paired the compulsion, regardless of what kind you use, with relief. Relief from pain, be it physical or emotional pain, is a powerful reinforcer for behaviour, which is, in some ways, how addiction can start. You can think of compulsions as an addiction of sorts, as the rush of relief floods your nervous system with a wave of the reward-hormone dopamine. This same hormone is released when we anticipate a reward, consume drugs or alcohol, and achieve a goal or objective.)
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
672 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 217 0 R
/T (Naturally, our brains like to feel good, so the next time it kicks off the OCD cycle with an intrusive brain message, you can almost view as your brain's way of attempting to get its next 'hit', so to speak. Pair the metaphor for addiction with the fear of anxiety, and you have a pretty good explanation for why you feel you can't stop.  )
/E (Naturally, our brains like to feel good, so the next time it kicks off the OCD cycle with an intrusive brain message, you can almost view as your brain's way of attempting to get its next 'hit', so to speak. Pair the metaphor for addiction with the fear of anxiety, and you have a pretty good explanation for why you feel you can't stop.  )
