/Title (Generally speaking, OCD is  characterised by two broad features, with the first, obsessions, accounting for unwanted experiences intruding)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2089 0 R
/Prev 2104 0 R
>>
endobj
2098 0 obj
<<
/Title (into consciousness that may be experienced as urges, images or thoughts, and the second, compulsions, accounting for later automatic behaviours that are used to reduce the resulting emotional discomfort, which is typically experienced as anxiety.)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2083 0 R
/Prev 2089 0 R
/Next 2105 0 R
>>
endobj
2099 0 obj
<<
/Title (Parenting and parental modelling are also thought to influence symptoms, in that children of parents with symptoms or symptom-adjacent tendencies may adopt traits or characteristics such as anxiety-sensitivity and anxiety-avoidance. Moreover, fixations on orderliness and rigid procedures, even in the absence of sense or logic, can be passed on in this way also.)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2083 0 R
/Prev 2106 0 R
/Next 2090 0 R
>>
endobj
2100 0 obj
<<
/Title (to the development of OCD. Individuals with trait shyness, emotionality, and behavioural inhibition tend to exhibit higher degrees of OCD symptoms.)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
