/Title (Serotonin \(5-HT\): Serotonin is perhaps the most extensively studied neurotransmitter in the context of OCD. Evidence suggests that dysregulation of the serotonin system, particularly reduced levels of serotonin, is associated with the pathophysiology of OCD \(Albert et al., 2020\). Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors \(SSRIs\), which increase serotonin levels in the brain, are commonly used as first-line pharmacological treatments for OCD, further supporting the serotonin hypothesis of OCD.)
/Dest [18 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2115 0 R
/Prev 2119 0 R
/Next 2126 0 R
>>
endobj
2126 0 obj
<<
/Title (Glutamate: Glutamate is the primary excitatory neurotransmitter in the brain and has been implicated in the neurobiology of OCD. Dysfunctions in glutamatergic neurotransmission, specifically abnormalities in glutamate levels or receptor functioning, have been observed in individuals with OCD \(Fineberg et al., 2020\). Emerging research suggests that targeting the glutamatergic system may offer novel therapeutic avenues for the treatment of OCD.)
/Dest [18 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2115 0 R
/Prev 2125 0 R
