2180 0 obj
<<
/Title (The following theoretical model by Wilhelm and Steketee \(2006\) provides a succinct overview for how OCD can form, and how symptoms are maintained. Recalling the earlier points on the genetic factors influencing the disorder's development, we can see that these factors combine with life experiences and personality traits/core beliefs to shape our interpretations of the intrusive thoughts we experience. To a greater extent, our interpretations of obsessive thoughts are what begins and sustains our suffering, which is why a substantial component of therapy is centered on changing our responses to thoughts we've labelled, or 'flagged', as dangerous and unacceptable.)
/Dest [24 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2173 0 R
/Next 2188 0 R
>>
endobj
2181 0 obj
<<
/Title (According to the model, personality traits \(such as perfectionism\)  influence the frequency and intensity of intrusive thoughts, and  mould our natural inclinations to respond to them in certain ways. After we've labelled a particular thought as harmful, unacceptable, or outright 'wrong', this typically produces a strong negative emotional reaction, or series of negative emotional reactions, including anxiety, dread, sadness, and depression. To regulate these emotions, that is, to attempt to get rid of or reduce them, we then engage in any number of compulsions or safety behaviours, which usually alleviate some immediate distress at the cost of our long-term recovery.)
/Dest [24 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2173 0 R
/Prev 2189 0 R
/First 2190 0 R
/Last 2190 0 R
/Count -1
>>
endobj
