/Title (As can be seen from Figure 3., the defusion strategies of ACT help to intervene between intrusive thoughts and any subsequent anxiety by inserting a kind-of mental 'circuit-breaker'. This circuit breaker reminds an individual that an intrusive thought is just that, a thought, and in no way holds any value or meaning. While this more adaptive response to intrusive thoughts can feel clunky, strange, and even pointless at first, what one can expect with repeated practice is an increased limiting of the extent to which intrusive thoughts spiral into anxiety-ridden rumination. Defusion techniques which can be used in response to intrusive thoughts will be discussed in detail throughout Module 2.)
/Dest [27 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2184 0 R
/Next 2196 0 R
>>
endobj
2196 0 obj
<<
/Title (Secondly, nominating and engaging in values-based behaviours can provide a behavioural circuit breaker to disrupt the impulse, or automatic tendency, to engage in compulsions to reduce anxiety. When combined, these strategies interrupt the often entrenched cycle of intrusive thought, anxiety, compulsion,  and relief.)
/Dest [27 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2184 0 R
/Prev 2195 0 R
>>
endobj
