2264 0 obj
<<
/Title (The funfamental way in which ACT can help us to disentangle our identification from our thinking is through defusion. Defusion is the act \(pun intended!\) of putting distance between our observing selves and the thoughts and feelings we encounter minute-by-minute. Perhaps the simplest way to summarise this practice is respond to the thoughts we encounter, rather than responding from them.)
/Dest [30 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2252 0 R
/Prev 2279 0 R
>>
endobj
2265 0 obj
<<
/Title (I totally get that this concept might seem a little strange at first, but if you stop for a moment and start to 'watch' your mind in motion, you'll notice sea of thoughts, impressions, beliefs and perceptions. You can watch them like clouds in the sky, or ducks upon a lake, and yet, they aren't 'you'. For all intents and purposes, you are the watcher observing the activity of your mind.)
/Dest [31 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2253 0 R
/Next 2280 0 R
>>
endobj
2266 0 obj
<<
/Title (To illustrate the point further, imagine you are the CEO \(observer self\) of a company \(your brain\). The CEO's job is take in all available information from within the company, and to decide on how the company will spend it's resources, solve problems and maximise profits. Advisors, managers and employees \(brain messages\) meet regularly with the CEO to report said information, and to offer insights as to the wellbeing of the company. However, the CEO is free to take the offered information or to reject it. The same goes for your brain and the thoughts it likes to gives you. You are free to take on thoughts, feelings and sensations as  your identity, or you are free to practice defusion.)
/Dest [31 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2253 0 R
