<<
/Title (This is reflected in our neurology, too, with our prefrontal cortex \(front of the brain\) acting as the seat of this observing self. While various other brain structures relay messages to the observer self by way of thoughts, sensations and emotions,  it is the observer self's job to decide which of these messages are valid, and which are unworthy of our precious energy and attention. Obsessive thoughts, while intrusive and uncomfortable, are merely another 'message' sent to us from our brains.)
/Dest [31 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2253 0 R
/Prev 2265 0 R
/Next 2281 0 R
>>
endobj
2281 0 obj
<<
/Title (We get into trouble when we start to demonise a particular message as horrible, unacceptable or somehow reflective of our 'true selves', which is how particular thoughts become paired so strongly with overwhelming anxiety. Essentially, our task in therapy is to 'reverse engineer' this process of labelling particular thoughts as unacceptably bad, and to instead treat all thoughts, whether intrusive or not, as neutral fragments of information that we are free to entertain or ignore.)
/Dest [31 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2253 0 R
/Prev 2280 0 R
/Next 2266 0 R
>>
endobj
